method:
I worked geometry first in prims.pkl (bank1, area L1) and did not look at any other labeller's or detector's output.

1. I listed every INSERT: 16 A-DOOR blocks, 11 'Basic Wall _ GLASS WALL 2600H 20X100' blocks, and the curtain-wall panels and mullions. Mirrored inserts carry OCS insert points (x is negated), so I took every position from the flattened geometry, not from 'ins'.
2. I checked the conversion. A dwgread JSON dump of I1201 has the same counts as plan.dxf for every A-WALL, A-DOOR and A-GLAZ entity type, including 16 door inserts.
3. In shapely I built wall polygons from the A-WALL / A-WALL-PATT hatches, the polygonised A-WALL / A-GLAZ / S-COLS lines and the column hatches. I then (a) paired facing wall end faces on the same line (gap 0.25..3.2 m, free path between them) and (b) cast rays from every short wall end face to find ends that face a wall or open space.
4. I matched every candidate against each A-DOOR block's frame, leaf and arc lines and the wall end coordinates. I took a and b at the bounding wall-end faces on the wall centre line. For doors in solid walls the A-DOOR-FRAM frames lie inside the opening. For doors in glazed screens and curtain walls, the jamb mullions count as the frame, and the clear widths are in the notes.
5. I made visual checks on:
   - the overview and my own full render, L1_full (grid 1, inserts);
   - 15 tiles of 5.2 x 5.2 m (grid 0.5, inserts, 2000 px): x -6.6..-1.4, -1.8..3.4 and 2.8..7.0 against y -9.8..-4.6, -5.0..0.2, -0.2..5.0, 4.6..9.8 and 9.4..13.0;
   - zooms of the back-room gap and the NE ATM gap;
   - a final full render with a marker at every opening (L1_marked);
   - end-point zooms of the SE lobby, the NE meeting / facade doors, the SW back rooms, the north corridor, the lift hall and the manager door.
   No unmarked door swing, leaf, door block or wall gap was left, apart from the rejected items.

The result is 16 openings: 15 doors (one of them the merged pair of pivot leaves) and 1 passage. There are no windows; all glazing is curtain wall or full-height glass partitions, listed under glassWalls. The machine-readable copy is key.json, and key.py rebuilds it.
